# Should everything be for sale?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why do people find some markets repugnant? Does paying for blood reduce how much is given?"
next: {"title":"Do markets change people?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/63-do-markets-change-people.cast"}

## Gift or sale?
sources:
    - id: roth2007
      title: Repugnance as a Constraint on Markets
      authors: Alvin E. Roth
      year: 2007
      finding: 'California banned selling horse meat for human consumption by referendum in 1998 (60 to 40). Concerns about monetizing transactions fall into three classes: objectification, coercion, slippery slope. Life insurance had to overcome initial repugnance in the early 1800s. Journal of Economic Perspectives 21(3): 37-58.'

    cards pick size 1.4 y 630 width 900 icon_side left bins ["For sale","Banned"] arrange rise check each hidden true
        * "A kidney" icon kidney bin Banned
        * "Your plasma" icon "drop of blood" bin "For sale"
        * "Horse steak" icon horse bin Banned
        * "A queue spot" icon ticket bin "For sale"
        * "Your vote" icon "ballot box" bin Banned
        * "Your eggs" icon egg bin "For sale"
    box h_sale "For sale" #2f6b8f width 270 height 64 font_size 38 in col_sale hidden true
    box c_plasma "Plasma" width 270 height 80 radius 10 icon "drop of blood" font_size 34 in col_sale hidden true
    box c_eggs "Eggs" width 270 height 80 radius 10 icon egg font_size 34 in col_sale hidden true
    box c_queue "Queue spot" width 270 height 80 radius 10 icon ticket font_size 34 in col_sale hidden true
    column col_sale gap 14 in board hidden true
    box h_ban "Banned" #b5482e width 270 height 64 font_size 38 in col_ban hidden true
    box c_kidney "Kidney" width 270 height 80 radius 10 icon kidney font_size 34 in col_ban hidden true
    box c_vote "Vote" width 270 height 80 radius 10 icon "ballot box" font_size 34 in col_ban hidden true
    box c_horse "Horse steak" width 270 height 80 radius 10 icon horse font_size 34 in col_ban hidden true
    column col_ban gap 14 in board hidden true
    box w_obj "Objectification" #8a5fa8 width 270 height 80 radius 10 font_size 34 in col_worry hidden true
    box w_coe "Coercion" #8a5fa8 width 270 height 80 radius 10 font_size 34 in col_worry hidden true
    box w_slip "Slippery slope" #8a5fa8 width 270 height 80 radius 10 font_size 34 in col_worry hidden true
    column col_worry gap 14 in board hidden true
    row board gap 50 align start fit.h 385 fit.w 940 fit.x 30 fit.y 260 hidden true
    text rep "Repugnant" #b5482e cites ["roth2007"] x 500 y 225 font_size 40 hidden true
    text rep_def "willing buyer, willing seller, others object" #8f887c x 500 y 185 font_size 28 hidden true
    text gift "Give it: hero ✓" #4a8a4a x 830 y 520 font_size 32 hidden true
    text sell "Sell it: crime ✗" #b5482e x 830 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    text insure "Life insurance: 1800s repugnant → today ordinary" #2f6b8f cites ["roth2007"] x 500 y 185 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Should everything be for sale?"

Should everything be for sale? Here are six things people would gladly buy or sell. Which of them can you legally sell in California?
    draw pick

B: Easy. Plasma and kidneys, surely not. Horse steak, obviously yes.
    pause 0.3

    ask "Which of these can you legally sell in California? Sort each card." on pick stage own store srt
        right: {srt} of {srt.total} on the first try.
        wrong: {srt} of {srt.total} on the first try: of these, only the kidney, the vote and the horse steak are banned.

    erase pick

Plasma, eggs and a spot in a queue: legal, and paid.
    draw h_sale c_plasma c_eggs c_queue parallel false

A kidney, a vote, and, in California, a horse steak: banned.
    draw h_ban c_kidney c_vote c_horse

B: Horse meat? Whose life does my steak ruin?
    highlight c_horse effect circle

Nobody's. California's voters banned selling it in 1998, sixty to forty, not to protect d (@draw rep ends@)iners, but because they found it repugnant that anyone would eat a horse.

B: The economist Alvin Roth, who won a Nobel for designing markets, calls this repugnance: a d (@draw rep_def@)eal that two people want, and others don't want them to make.

And often it's the money that offends, not the act.
    highlight c_kidney

B: Give a kid (@draw gift@)ney to your sister and you're a hero. Sell one to  (@draw sell@)a stranger and you've committed a crime.

    erase gift sell

Roth sorts the worries into three. Object (@draw w_obj@)ification: a price turns something precious into a mere thing.

B: Coercion: big money can push the poorest into deals they'd never choose otherwise.
    draw w_coe

And the slippery slope: allow this, and something worse follows.
    draw w_slip

    erase rep_def

B: Repugnance moves, though. In the ea (@draw insure@)rly 1800s, life insurance looked like a bet on your own death. Today it's ordinary.

    erase insure

So a ban is about meaning and fairness, not about harm to bystanders. Whether a price really changes what people give, we can test, with blood.
    highlight col_worry effect box

## Does a price drive out the gift?
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation:
      - y = 30 + b*x
      - y = 30 + b*x - c*(1 - exp(-x/5))
    params:
      b: {value: 0.5, fixed: true}
      c: {value: 0, min: 0, max: 25, step: 1, label: crowding out c}
    editable:
      - c
    controls: equation
    drag: c
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 60
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 60
    x_label: Payment ($)
    y_label: Donors per 100
    marks:
      - {kind: hline, at: 30, label: unpaid}
    box:
      x: 360
      'y': 110
      w: 630
      h: 540
sources:
    - id: titmuss1970
      title: 'The Gift Relationship: From Human Blood to Social Policy'
      authors: Richard M. Titmuss
      year: 1970
      finding: Compared England and Wales (all voluntary donors) with the United States (many paid donors); argued that paying for blood reduced supply and quality (more hepatitis) and drove out altruism.
    - id: arrow1972
      title: Gifts and Exchanges
      authors: Kenneth J. Arrow
      year: 1972
      finding: 'Philosophy & Public Affairs 1(4): 343-362. Doubted that adding the option of payment would reduce altruistic giving, and found Titmuss gave no evidence for it.'
    - id: benabou2006
      title: Incentives and Prosocial Behavior
      authors: Roland Bénabou & Jean Tirole
      year: 2006
      finding: 'American Economic Review 96(5): 1652-1678. Rewards for prosocial acts can weaken their value as a signal of one''s good character (image motivation), so incentives can backfire.'

    text titmuss "Richard Titmuss, 1970:\nBritain vs America" #3d3833 cites ["titmuss1970"] x 175 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text tclaim "Titmuss:\npay → less blood,\nmore hepatitis" #b5482e cites ["titmuss1970"] x 165 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text aclaim "Arrow, 1972:\nmoney can\nonly add" #2f6b8f cites ["arrow1972"] x 165 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    label lab0 "Price effect" above #2f6b8f attach_to curve_0 hidden true
    text lab1 "With crowding out" #b5482e at.data [40,12] font_size 26 hidden true
    text illus "illustrative" #8f887c x 165 y 160 font_size 22 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"skipmath"}] then skipmath hidden true
    scratch mcard cites ["benabou2006"] work [{"tex":"\\text{give if } a + p + R > \\text{cost}"},{"tex":"p>0:\\ R \\text{ falls}"},{"tex":"\\text{small } p:\\ \\text{lost } R > p"},{"tex":"\\text{large } p:\\ p \\text{ wins}"}] x 500 y 400 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Does a price drive out the gift?"

    hide equations

Blood is where this argument got its sharpest test. In 1970 the British social researcher Richard Titmuss compared Britain, where every donor gave for free, with America, where many were paid.
    draw axes titmuss

    erase titmuss

B: And his verdict? Paying was (@draw tclaim@) worse. Less blood was given, and more of it carried hepatitis.

    erase tclaim

Here's the economist's first reply, with ill (@draw curve_0 lab0 illus@)ustrative numbers. A payment is a price, and a higher price brings out more supply.

B: Kenneth Arrow made exactl (@draw aclaim@)y that point in 1972. Anyone who wants to give for free still can. Adding money can only add donors.

    erase aclaim

Titmuss thought the money changes what the act means. Once blood (@draw curve_1@) has a price, giving it is no longer a gift, just a cheap sale.

    draw hline

(@draw lab1@) Then the curve sags. A small payment loses the people who gave out of kindness, and only a big one wins back more than it lost.
    animate params.c.value 20 duration 3.5

B: There's a sharper version. If people might think you gave for the money, giving no longer shows you're a good person.
    highlight curve_1

Try it: drag the red curve, and find where a small payment does worse than paying nothing at all.
    explore

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: A short detour, if you like.

@math

    erase mathq

    fade axes curve_0 curve_1 hline lab0 lab1 illus to 0.12

Say you give if your warm glow a, plus the payment p, plus your image R, beats the cost.
    draw mcard_box mcard_line_1

B: Pay anything, and onlookers can't tell why you gave, so the image R drops.
    draw mcard_line_2

For a small payment, the image lost is bigger than p, and fewer give. F (@draw mcard_line_4 ends@)or a large one, p wins.
    draw mcard_line_3

    erase mcard

    fade axes curve_0 curve_1 hline lab0 lab1 illus to 1

@skipmath

    erase mathq

Both curves are stories, and each one hangs together. Which one blood is on, only data can tell.
    highlight curve_0 curve_1

## What the blood says
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - No pay
      - Paid 50 kr
      - Pay to charity
    values:
      - 52
      - 30
      - 53
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 60
    y_label: Women who came (%)
    colors:
      - null
      - red
      - green
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 130
      w: 470
      h: 510
sources:
    - id: mellstrom2008
      title: 'Crowding Out in Blood Donation: Was Titmuss Right?'
      authors: Carl Mellström & Magnus Johannesson
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Field experiment in Gothenburg: offering 50 SEK cut the share of women who came for the donor health examination from 52% to 30%; when the payment could be given to charity it was 53%; no significant effect for men. Journal of the European Economic Association 6(4): 845-863.'
    - id: lacetera2013
      title: Economic Rewards to Motivate Blood Donations
      authors: Nicola Lacetera, Mario Macis & Robert Slonim
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Science 340: 927-928. Review of field studies in the US, Argentina, Switzerland and Italy: 18 of 19 incentive items raised donations; no detected effect on blood safety where measured; some displacement across drives and in timing, no long-run negative effects.'
    - id: fda1978
      title: 'Labeling of blood and blood components: paid or volunteer donor (43 FR 2142)'
      authors: US Food and Drug Administration
      year: 1978
      finding: From 15 May 1978, blood for transfusion must be labelled 'paid donor' or 'volunteer donor', to reduce transfusion-associated hepatitis.
    - id: plasma
      title: Estimates of the US share of plasma collected for medicines (industry and policy sources, e.g. Policy Options / IRPP 2020)
      authors: various
      year: 2020
      finding: The US, which pays plasma donors, supplies roughly two thirds or more (65-70%) of the world's plasma for plasma-derived medicines.
    - id: flynn2011
      title: Flynn v. Holder, 684 F.3d 852 (9th Cir. 2012)
      authors: US Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit
      year: 2011
      finding: Paying donors of bone-marrow stem cells collected by apheresis is not barred by the National Organ Transplant Act; the plaintiffs planned $3,000 awards as a scholarship, housing subsidy or gift to a charity of the donor's choice.
    - id: roth2007
      title: Repugnance as a Constraint on Markets
      authors: Alvin E. Roth
      year: 2007
      finding: 'Objections to monetizing transactions: objectification, coercion, slippery slope. Journal of Economic Perspectives 21(3): 37-58.'

    box k1 "Pay cuts donors" width 420 height 74 radius 10 font_size 32 in claims hidden true
    text v1 "18 of 19: more donors" below k1 gap 12 #2f6b8f font_size 28 hidden true
    box k2 "Paid blood less safe" width 420 height 74 radius 10 font_size 32 in claims hidden true
    text v2 "then: yes. now: no" below k2 gap 12 #2f6b8f font_size 28 hidden true
    box k3 "Money spoils the gift" width 420 height 74 radius 10 font_size 32 in claims hidden true
    text v3 "sometimes ✓" below k3 gap 12 #b5482e font_size 28 hidden true
    column claims gap 70 fit.h 430 fit.w 460 fit.x 520 fit.y 190 hidden true
    mark strike1 around k1 kind strike hidden true
    text big "18 of 19\nrewards:\nmore donors" #2f6b8f cites ["lacetera2013"] x 255 y 420 font_size 46 hidden true
    text plasma_cue "Plasma:\nUS pays donors,\n≈ 2/3 of world supply" #8a5fa8 cites ["plasma"] x 255 y 420 font_size 34 hidden true
    text s1 "Rewards raise giving:\nmostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 255 y 520 font_size 32 hidden true
    text s2 "Cash for blood:\ncontested" #d0865f x 255 y 390 font_size 32 hidden true
    text s3 "Should it be for sale?\nopen" #b5482e x 255 y 260 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "What the blood says"

Theory can't settle it, so economists went out and offered donors money.
    draw k1 k2 k3

B: In Sweden, Carl Mellström and Magnus Johannesso (@draw axes bar_1@)n invited people to come in for the donor health check. Some were offered fifty kronor. How many women came?

    ask "Drag the 'Paid 50 kr' bar to your guess." on bar_2 store g voice b
        right: Thirty percent.
        wrong: It was thirty percent.

Among women, the share who came fell from fifty-two to thirty percent. Men barely changed.
    highlight bar_2

B: So Titmuss was right? But when the women could hand the fifty kr (@draw bar_3@)onor to charity, the share went back to fifty-three.

So the money matte (@draw v3@)red for what it said, not for what it paid.

    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

But bigger tests with real donors point the other way. Nicola Lacetera, Mario Macis and Robert Slonim review (@draw big@)ed rewards in four countries, and eighteen of nineteen (@draw strike1@) raised donations.

B: Mostly gift card (@draw v1@)s, T-shirts and lottery tickets, not cash. And no sign the blood got less safe, though some donors simply switched from one drive to another.

    erase big

The safety worry was real in Titmuss's day (@draw v2@): paid blood carried more hepatitis, and since 1978 American blood is labelled paid or volunteer.

And plasma? America pays its do (@draw plasma_cue@)nors and supplies about two thirds of the world's plasma for medicines.

    erase plasma_cue

So how sure are we? That rewards raise blo (@draw s1@)od donations: mostly agreed. That cash for whole blood  (@draw s2@)does no harm: contested.

B: And whether a kidney or a vote should ever be for sale, no study can settl (@draw s3@)e. That's a question of values, and it stays open.

    quiz "A US group proposed paying bone-marrow donors $3,000 as a scholarship, housing help, or a gift to a charity of their choice. Going by the Swedish experiment, why does the charity option matter?" voice b
        intro: Three new cases to finish.
        right: It lets donors who'd feel bought still give: in Sweden, the charity option brought the women back.
        wrong: In Sweden, sending the money to charity brought the women back: here, it lets donors who'd feel bought still give.
        + It lets donors who'd feel bought still give
        * It makes the reward bigger
        * It avoids tax

    hide k1 k2 k3 v1 v2 v3 strike1 s1 s2 s3

    cards worries y 620 width 900 bins ["Coercion","Objectification","Slippery slope"] arrange rise check each
        Coercion: Paying the poor to test drugs
        Objectification: A price list for adopted babies
        * "Paid plasma now, paid kidneys next" bin "Slippery slope"

    ask "Sort each case under the worry it raises." on worries stage own store mw
        right: All three: pressure on the poor is coercion, a price on a child is objectification, and one sale leading to the next is the slippery slope.
        wrong: {mw} of {mw.total} on the first try. Pressure on the poor is coercion, pricing what should be a gift objectification, one sale leading to the next the slippery slope.

    erase worries

    show k1 k2 k3 v1 v2 v3 strike1 s1 s2 s3

    quiz "A museum short of weekend volunteer guides gives each one a T-shirt and a free family pass. What does the blood-drive evidence predict?"
        right: More volunteers, some moved from other volunteer jobs: small gifts raised giving in eighteen of nineteen blood drives, with some switching.
        wrong: Small gifts raised giving in eighteen of nineteen blood drives, though some donors only switched: here, more volunteers, some moved from other jobs.
        + More volunteers, some moved from other volunteer jobs
        * Fewer volunteers, as the kind ones quit
        * No change: volunteers ignore gifts

So should everything be for sale? The evidence tells us what a price does, often more good than its critics feared. What we're willing to put a price on is still ours to decide.
    highlight s3

